## Solution

The residue $\mathrm{Res}_s(\eta)$ is an element of the residue field $k(s)$, not of $k$ in general. These coincide only when $s$ is a $k$-rational point.

### Proof

**Setup and local structure at $s$.** Let $s \in S$ be a closed point. The local ring $\mathcal{O}_{\bar{C},s}$ is a discrete valuation ring with uniformizer $t$ and residue field $k(s)$, a finite extension of $k$. Its completion is $\hat{\mathcal{O}}_{\bar{C},s} \cong k(s)[[t]]$, with fraction field $K_s = k(s)((t))$.

**Definition of the residue.** A rational section $\eta$ of $\Omega^1_{\bar{C}/k}(\log S)$ is, at the generic point, an element of the one-dimensional $k(\bar{C})$-vector space $\Omega^1_{k(\bar{C})/k}$. Locally at $s$, we may write
$$\eta = f(t)\,\frac{dt}{t}, \qquad f(t) \in k(s)((t)),$$
where $dt/t$ is the standard local generator of $\Omega^1(\log S)$ at the logarithmic point $s$ (this is the natural local frame: $\Omega^1(\log S)$ is locally free of rank 1 with generator $dt/t$ near $s$). Expanding $f(t) = \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} a_n t^n$ with $a_n \in k(s)$, the residue is
$$\mathrm{Res}_s(\eta) := a_{-1} \in k(s).$$

**Why the residue lands in $k(s)$, not $k$.** The coefficients $a_n$ of the Laurent expansion of $f$ lie in $k(s)$ because the completed local field is $k(s)((t))$—the coefficient field of the Laurent series ring is the residue field $k(s)$, not the base field $k$. The coefficient $a_{-1}$ is therefore an element of $k(s)$. There is no canonical way to identify $a_{-1}$ with an element of $k$ unless $k(s) = k$ (i.e., $s$ is $k$-rational) or a specific embedding/trace is chosen.

**The log-geometric residue map confirms this.** For the sheaf of logarithmic differentials on a smooth curve, there is the standard exact sequence
$$0 \to \Omega^1_{\bar{C}/k} \to \Omega^1_{\bar{C}/k}(\log S) \xrightarrow{\mathrm{Res}} \mathcal{O}_S \to 0,$$
where $\mathcal{O}_S$ is the structure sheaf of the reduced divisor $S$. The stalk of $\mathcal{O}_S$ at $s$ is exactly $k(s)$. Thus the residue map, evaluated at $s$, is a map
$$\mathrm{Res}_s : \Omega^1_{\bar{C}/k}(\log S)_s \longrightarrow k(s),$$
confirming that the residue is valued in $k(s)$. For a global section of $\Omega^1(\log S)$, this is exact. For a rational section (allowing higher-order poles), the same local computation above defines the residue, and it still lands in $k(s)$.

**The role of the trace.** The reason one might mistakenly think the residue lies in $k$ is the residue theorem, which in this generality reads
$$\sum_{s \in \bar{C}} \mathrm{Tr}_{k(s)/k}\bigl(\mathrm{Res}_s(\eta)\bigr) = 0.$$
The trace map $\mathrm{Tr}_{k(s)/k} : k(s) \to k$ is needed to bring the individual residues (each in its own $k(s)$) back to the common ground field $k$ so that the sum makes sense. This application of the trace is precisely necessary because each $\mathrm{Res}_s(\eta)$ lives in $k(s)$, not in $k$.

**Conclusion.** The residue $\mathrm{Res}_s(\eta)$ is an element of the residue field $k(s)$. It lies in $k$ if and only if $s$ is $k$-rational (i.e., $k(s) = k$).

